The Witcher 3 hearts of stone is that  once a horror story of romance a  character study and a classic fairy tale  and somehow these disparate parts mesh  to form a cohesive whole  this is CD Projekt RED first paid  expansion for it's masterful RPG and it  fits seamlessly into gerelds narrative  although it's meant for late-game  characters around level 30 it's  available at any point in your  playthrough and like many experiences in  The Witcher 3 the expansions main draw  as it's twisting storylines and vivid  characters it's difficult to talk about  these elements without ruining the  experience as a whole CD Projekt RED  presents its own dark take on weddings  haunted houses and the story of the  prince and the Frog quest never resolved  how I expected them to and seldom how I  hope hearts of stone runs the gamut of  fantasy tropes but subverts everyone  it's this willingness to take successful  risks that sets these quests and the  story they compose apart many of these  storylines trace back to one man gone  taro dim Geralt calls in the merchant of  mirrors others know him as the man of  glass he's a character who makes you  feel uneasy with a glance and even when  you seem relaxed I always had my thumb  hovering over the attack button but  there are good people in the northern  kingdoms too however few and far between  hearts of stone reintroduces Shawnee the  medic from oxenfurt who reprises her  role from the original witcher she's my  favorite character here and maybe in all  of the witcher 3  she's equal parts charming caring and  clever in fact Shani's much smarter than  Geralt your responses have a time limit  much more frequently when speaking with  her she's quick-witted but only gives  that away the more time you spend with  her and if you don't remember or weren't  aware of the history that garel and her  share from the earlier games it doesn't  matter  CD Projekt RED conveys this with expert  dialogue and subtle visual cues it's not  often you can read into animated  characters body language but here  Shaunie's lingering glances and Geralt  sluzer gestures tell a story in  themselves once again CD Projekt RED  excels at capturing the details between  these living stories and fluid  narratives are engrossing quests I  planned a heist recruited team members  and cringed as my plan fell apart I also  attended a wedding straight out of a  sitcom complete with praiseworthy comedy  and awkward scenarios  it's almost midnight time for the  captain wait I'm not done oh yes you are  even in the absence of combat and  supernatural catastrophes hearts of  stone pulled me through with expert  pacing well for the most part at least  CD Projekt implemented several minigames  throughout this expansion most of which  are tracking sections Geralt Witcher  senses usually lead you only a few feet  away to a break of a wall or hidden  object that seemed place just for the  sake of it there's also an infuriating  section that places you in a muddy pen  as you try to herd pigs toward their  respective troughs the activity alone  breaks the overall immersion but Geralt  wide turns double the annoyance factor  in the end these activities are  negligible in the grand scheme of things  when combat does rear its head there are  unique enemies and tenacious boss  battles to keep the dynamism alive in  fact I saw three such enemies within the  first two hours of hearts of stone I  fought a mage a headless swordsman and a  hideous beast much largely garel with  more complex attack patterns and many of  the northern kingdoms other denizens  there's this one enemy later on in a  graveyard outside an abandoned Manor  whose character design echoes the  horrors of Pan's Labyrinth it's  grotesque it's terrifying stabilities  make for one of the more grueling  encounters I faced as Jerrold defeating  that thing was both a triumph and a  relief for all of its storylines and all  of its varied combat hearts of stone  houses deeper themes ideas of regret  apathy death and the passage of time  pervade every characters motivations  this thematic pulse elevates an already  great batch of content parts of stone  feels just as much a part of the  narrative as any of the main games quest  lines but it also stands on its own as a  memorable adventure it may have its  negative moments and the pacing may be  broken at certain intervals but The  Witcher 3 is first paid expansion it's  well worth the return to the northern  kingdoms  let's scooch along kitty this place  what's that  that ticking  you
 

